 # Ray Traversal Algorithms
 
-Also called: Hierarchical Ray Traversal, Octree Ray Casting
+Also called: Hierarchical Ray Traversal, Octree Ray Casting, Sparse Voxel Octree Traversal
 
 ## Overview
-Ray traversal algorithms determine the sequence of nodes in a spatial hierarchy (typically an octree) that a viewing ray intersects, enabling efficient volume rendering by skipping empty space. Unlike uniform grid ray marching, which samples every voxel, these methods use hierarchical descent to jump over large empty regions, significantly reducing computational cost for sparse datasets [S1, S6, S8, unofficial]. They differ from sibling methods like [Sparse Voxel Octree Ray Traversal](2451-sparse-voxel-octree-ray-traversal.md) in their specific handling of implicit vs. explicit structures, and from [Stackless Traversal](5202-stackless-traversal.md) in their reliance on explicit backtracking or specific recursive descent logic.
+Ray traversal algorithms determine the sequence of nodes or voxels intersected by a ray in a hierarchical spatial data structure (typically an octree or sparse voxel octree) to accelerate volume rendering or ray tracing. They solve the problem of efficiently skipping empty space and reducing the number of memory accesses required to sample volumetric data. This family is distinguished from sibling methods like [Stackless Traversal](5202-stackless-traversal.md) by its reliance on explicit or implicit stack-based descent/ascent logic to manage irregular tree structures, and from [Sparse Voxel Octree Ray Traversal](2451-sparse-voxel-octree-ray-traversal.md) by the broader scope of optimization techniques (e.g., rational arithmetic, presence acceleration) applied to the traversal path itself.
 
