 ## Background
-The concept of using hierarchical structures to accelerate ray tracing was established in the mid-1980s. The ARTS (Accelerated Ray-Tracing System) project introduced 3DDDA, a 3D line generator for efficient traversal of spatial enumeration and hybrid octree structures, providing an order of magnitude improvement in speed over previous methods [S3]. Later, Wald et al. developed optimized single-ray octree algorithms for large volume data, noting that coherent techniques like frustum-based traversal could degrade performance on highly variant scenes [S1]. More recently, techniques like Presence Acceleration and Rational Ray Parameterization have refined traversal robustness and efficiency for specific hardware and precision requirements [S1][S2, unofficial][S3].
+The concept of using hierarchical structures for ray traversal originated in the early days of ray tracing to handle complex scenes. The 3DDDA algorithm, introduced in the 1980s, was one of the first to use spatial enumeration and hybrid octrees to accelerate ray-object intersection, providing an order of magnitude speedup over naive methods [S4]. In the context of volume rendering, the use of octrees to skip transparent voxels became prominent with the work of Wald et al. (2006), who demonstrated that octrees could significantly reduce memory bandwidth requirements for large isosurface ray tracing [S1]. The Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) format, popularized by Laine and Karras (2010) and others, further optimized this by storing only occupied nodes, enabling efficient GPU-based traversal [S2, S6, S9, unofficial]. Rational parameterization methods were developed to address precision issues in floating-point traversal, ensuring robust neighbor finding in arbitrary view configurations [S1]. Presence acceleration and boundary cell techniques offer alternative pointerless or pre-computed acceleration structures for specific hardware or rendering constraints [S1][S2, S3, unofficial][S1].
 
